{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2403 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5badd8ce0df06057402358d09b8ae7edba2247197da0c685a13b7900a6a26e26" :edb/db-before-t 2402 :edb/db-after-t 2403 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535715 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:49:11.332Z" 13194139535715 true] [17592186197976 1100 "The lamina $D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\le x \\le y  ,\\, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139535715 true] [17592186197976 1100 "The lamina $D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le y,\\, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y$. Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139535715 false] [17592186197976 1145 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{y} &= ∬_(D)x \\cdot x^{2}ydA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x^{3}ydA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{y}x^{3}ydxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}y[∫_{0}^{y}x^{3}dx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}y \\cdot [\\frac{x^{4}}{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}y \\cdot \\frac{y^{4}}{4}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{y^{5}}{4}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{y^{6}}{24}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 false] [17592186197976 1145 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y .$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{5}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{6}}{24}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186197977 1100 "A thin plate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/3cfabe3c702883ca24c3188908c4cefeee43adb30797308828edd98374b5b44f.png) with the mass density function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/ac38ea36432f3e278a4c2deb9203fbd5e486e348976e79157a5c6885a576f656.png) is enclosed between the curve ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/65b9fa371f35bfa31a21b50bc02f64561e4c838d71ebd99bf2d2d1c1be196dff.png) and the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/1fd42a46221d9624ec3d81db0f5c73cd8428ee2f8d80b5d7e4ff67e7018fb194.png)-axis  for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/11d74a99b97245c1998471b33f9b9d0974e251345f825c475db4079c02fa273a.png) Find the moment of the plate about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/b63bded3ee2ad150f576fd05c209caf32bd34853f4a7132fe4ee85b59b06f61a.png)-axis." 13194139535715 true] [17592186197977 1100 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y) = 2x$ is enclosed between the curve $y=1-x^2$ and the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$. Find the moment of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139535715 false] [17592186197977 1145 "The moment of a lamina ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) with the mass density function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/bb3bf01e45585dbd5fe7732a1befa050d734bc46a466fea7f61f42851cfd3af8.png) about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/d7d15efdd7573bd5b95e49ef05727ed1b8163a06781c393c1d3f27109fdc4eda.png)-axis is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/73a6e88adb05252ff114153227c13ae4f4adad9b676524179a20d9ae3d045d43.png)\n\nIn our case, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/33d4430552670d83a2c303e8ab286d3391f5611450f4e4f0b6ad6ccb58049b0e.png) is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/067543543031a1d8cb4ceb9a7901ad7db77629243adc6658d4a9eab3e98fe09f.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/03ccfab8b682725e94126f9fe59597d6e2886d3aeff64746681d0bbbed072520.png)\n\nand ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/17c342255c9bffbade441c02123bab3f7154e97b1a6a69a2874935f2dd684806.png) \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/ca38251cdd0a97967e8e6f4200e94b00571c5cfe2b84fef1991022e4fb976e62.png)" 13194139535715 true] [17592186197977 1145 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4166/Source/Images/7d803c99f95d817b7da87f748075ad7f.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le 1-x^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = 2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{x} &= ∬_(D)y \\cdot 2xdA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2xydA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1 - x^{2}}2xydydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}2x[∫_{0}^{1 - x^{2}}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}2x \\cdot [\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1 - x^{2}}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x(1 - x^{2})^{2}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x - 2x^{3} + x^{5}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2}x^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} + \\frac{1}{6}x^{6}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 false] [17592186198165 1100 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y) = x+y$ and is enclosed between the curve $y=x$ and the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$. Given that the mass of the plate is $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$, what is the value of $\\overline{y}$?" 13194139535715 false] [17592186198165 1100 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y) = x+y$ and is enclosed between the curve $y=x$ and the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ units and its center of mass is located at $( \\overline{x}, \\overline{y} ),$ what is the value of $\\overline{y}?$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186198165 1145 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4166/Source/Images/00dd64e1a9f10365c732090d6562d731.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\:0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = x+y\n$$\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{x} &= ∬_(D)yλ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)y \\cdot (x + y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)xy + y^{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}xy + y^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[\\frac{xy^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(\\frac{x^{3}}{2} + \\frac{x^{3}}{3})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}∫_{0}^{1}x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}[\\frac{1}{4}x^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{4} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{24}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$" 13194139535715 false] [17592186198165 1145 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/b872242423692a20a9d50658c99d9e8cee7c9b2737fb96592bc2bc35b4f72c32.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\:0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda(x,y) = x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}xy+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{24}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186198166 1100 "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $x=\\sqrt{1-y^2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1$. Find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139535715 false] [17592186198166 1100 "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $x=\\sqrt{1-y^2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139535715 true] [17592186198166 1145 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c)$, where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4166/Source/Images/d671a38e2ba00f523dee97b36289fe6a.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{1-y^2}, \\: -1 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nA_D = \\dfrac12 \\cdot \\pi\\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{c} &= \\frac{1}{A_{D}}∬_(D)xdA \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}}xdxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}∫_{-1}^{1}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{x = 0}^{x = \\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}∫_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1 - y^{2}}{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}∫_{-1}^{1}1 - y^{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}[y - \\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}[(1 - \\frac{1}{3}) - (-1 + \\frac{1}{3})] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3π}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 false] [17592186198166 1145 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c),$ where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \n\\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/a7992c54f85f85cab3d9f923b8daca62c410393b364d30bcfbf5cff5efc93bbd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{1-y^2}, \\: -1 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nA_D = \\dfrac12 \\cdot \\pi\\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[(1-\\frac{1}{3})-(-1+\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186198291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362964 13194139535715 true] [17592186198291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362971 13194139535715 true] [17592186198291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362978 13194139535715 true] [17592186198292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362985 13194139535715 true] [17592186198292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362992 13194139535715 true] [17592186198292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362999 13194139535715 true] [17592186198292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363006 13194139535715 true] [17592186198333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362929 13194139535715 true] [17592186198333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362936 13194139535715 true] [17592186198333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362943 13194139535715 true] [17592186198333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362950 13194139535715 true] [17592186198333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362957 13194139535715 true] [17592186198334 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362901 13194139535715 true] [17592186198334 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362908 13194139535715 true] [17592186198334 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362915 13194139535715 true] [17592186198334 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362922 13194139535715 true] [17592186198556 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535715 true] [17592186198556 1100 "The lamina $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le y \\le x, 0 \\le x \\le 1}$ has the mass density function $λ(x, y) = 3xy$. Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139535715 false] [17592186198556 1100 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x,\\,0\\le x\\le 1}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=3xy.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139535715 true] [17592186198556 1145 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 3xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186198889 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535715 true] [17592186198889 1100 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le x^{2}}$ and has the mass density function $λ(x, y) = 4xy$. Given that the mass of the lamina is $21$ units and its center of mass is located at $(x^(―), y^(―))$, what is the value of $y^(―)$?" 13194139535715 false] [17592186198889 1100 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=4xy.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $21$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186198889 1145 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{8}}{6}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{6}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{85}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{85}{2})}{21}=\\frac{85}{42}.\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186198901 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535715 true] [17592186198901 1100 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2,-x \\le y \\le x}$. Given that the area of the lamina is $4$ square units, find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139535715 false] [17592186198901 1100 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-x\\le y\\le x}.$ Given that the area of the lamina is $4$ square units, find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139535715 true] [17592186198901 1145 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186198902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535715 true] [17592186198902 1100 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}$ and is enclosed between the curve $x=1-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{4}{15}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186198902 1100 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $λ(x, y) = y^{2}$ and is enclosed between the curve $x = 1 - y^{2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-1 \\le y \\le 1$. Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{4}{15}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(x^(―), y^(―))$, what is the value of $x^(―)$?" 13194139535715 false] [17592186198902 1145 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/78f9da3d48727d2cb3c447be0f4050a044c4aabe29179fd31f04cd7b6c66eabd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot (1-2{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{y}^{7}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{105}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{8}{105})}{(\\frac{4}{15})}=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362901 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362902 13194139535715 true] [17592186362901 1089 "q-162974" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362901 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535715 true] [17592186362901 1100 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{y}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139535715 true] [17592186362901 1113 #uuid "51f31efc-ef25-5078-b42e-7e203fa42644" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362901 1145 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{y}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{15{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}15{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}15{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}\\cdot [y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}\\cdot (1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}-15{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{5}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362903 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362904 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362905 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362906 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362907 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1041 "selection" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362905 13194139535715 true] [17592186362902 1110 #uuid "436898f8-dee1-536c-9ced-18fc1b017ff2" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362903 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362903 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362903 1111 #uuid "33240905-4701-5489-bad5-983396955edc" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362904 1034 "3" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362904 1111 #uuid "1ee50fad-1b9c-54a8-a718-d346dd6f26a7" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362905 1034 "2" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362905 1111 #uuid "f3f964cd-6b10-54ed-a99b-55fab9170c13" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362906 1034 "1" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362906 1111 #uuid "54f2e7bc-651e-5ee6-9c79-8bc293ed4e7f" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362907 1034 "\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362907 1111 #uuid "00c3bb50-dfa3-5735-9b29-b530faf81991" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362908 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362909 13194139535715 true] [17592186362908 1089 "q-162977" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362908 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535715 true] [17592186362908 1100 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{y}}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139535715 true] [17592186362908 1113 #uuid "e2ec72a2-bd84-5cac-b0f8-c87f50fac582" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362908 1145 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x={e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362910 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362911 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362912 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362913 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362914 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1041 "selection" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362913 13194139535715 true] [17592186362909 1110 #uuid "77731024-a26d-564c-b634-219e39134c97" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362910 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362910 1111 #uuid "20936872-cdc9-5591-971d-5ab262ba9b00" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362911 1034 "2" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362911 1111 #uuid "3dd80545-6788-5ad3-b0b8-22d02c729458" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362912 1034 "1" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362912 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362912 1111 #uuid "669280de-ebd8-562b-b86c-3e7e0fdf05e2" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362913 1034 "4" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362913 1111 #uuid "9e062b6c-5651-537e-b1aa-65d477891b42" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362914 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535715 true] [17592186362914 1111 #uuid "e12407bf-c792-5554-bef3-bb3758950025" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362915 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362916 13194139535715 true] [17592186362915 1089 "q-162984" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362915 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535715 true] [17592186362915 1100 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}$ is enclosed between the curve $y=2-{x}^{2}$ and  the line $y=1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139535715 true] [17592186362915 1113 #uuid "b4389be5-537b-5699-b760-014d4f425b90" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362915 1145 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068268/assets/2d08bcbbcd7ec4d8c388d24728c3f3afdc7c954497f342e9ac2b459fad24e5d2.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{3}{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2-{x}^{2}}3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3[y]_{y=1}^{y=2-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3[2-{x}^{2}-1]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3-3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x-{x}^{3}]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(3-1)-(-3+1) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535715 true] [17592186362916 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362917 13194139535715 true] [17592186362916 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362918 13194139535715 true] [17592186362916 1